# pkg/app conventions

`pkg/app` holds no code any more: this file is all that is left in it. The
LIVE composition root is **`internal/app`**, the composition graph, which every
process command in `cmd/<bin>/cmd/` runs:

```
internal/app/providers.go   yours: provider funcs New<X>(<X>Deps) (T, error) — the DB pool, the ORM,
                            clients, the auth chain, listeners
internal/app/compose.go     yours, reconciled: one g.new<X>() constructor method per node, holding
                            the value expression for every field of that node's Deps
internal/app/roots.go       yours (optional): //forge:root structs — what each process builds
internal/app/graph_gen.go   generated: memoized accessors g.<X>(), Build<Root>/Run<Root>,
                            the default Server root
internal/app/routes_gen.go  generated: <Audience>Routes route sets and per-service MountOptions
cmd/<bin>/cmd/roots_gen.go  generated: one command per root, handed to runRoot in the owned serve.go
```

Every part of a process is a node built from its `Deps`: a handler, a
package, a worker, an operator, and every provider. **Every `Deps` field
resolves by type**, at generate time, never by name: give a provider's value
its own type (`type DB struct{ *sql.DB }`) and any `Deps` field of that type is
filled from it. Accessors are memoized, so `g.Users()` builds once, and a root
builds only the nodes its fields reach. A value with `Run`, `Drain`, `Close` or
`Ready` methods is supervised: started in construction order, stopped in
reverse.

`compose.go` is YOURS. Forge writes it once, then reconciles only the
projection: a node with no `new<X>` method gets one, a `Deps` field a node
gained gets its key, and a key naming a field its `Deps` struct no longer has
is dropped. The EXPRESSION beside a key that still exists is never touched, so
changing what fills a field is an edit there. A field two nodes could fill is
a generate error until you write its key yourself. `forge project disown
internal/app/compose.go` stops reconciliation altogether.

To add a collaborator (a client, an adapter), add a provider in
`providers.go`; generate wires it wherever a `Deps` field has its type. The
architecture skill has the rest: roots and commands, per-root config reach,
and `forge lint --graph` (`forge skill load architecture`).

A project from before the graph (an owned `Infra` + `OpenInfra` +
`NewComponents`) keeps that composition until `forge fix composition-graph`
moves it. The migration support that used to live here is the generated
`db/embed_gen.go` (`db.Source()`), applied by `migratekit.AutoMigrate` from the
owned `cmd/<bin>/cmd/serve.go`.

## Where do per-RPC nil checks belong? (Spoiler: nowhere.)

Forge generates `New(Deps) (*Service, error)` and a `validateDeps()`
helper. Add your required fields to that single method:

```go
func (d Deps) validateDeps() error {
    if d.Logger == nil   { return fmt.Errorf("foo: Deps.Logger is required") }
    if d.Config == nil   { return fmt.Errorf("foo: Deps.Config is required") }
    if d.Repo == nil     { return fmt.Errorf("foo: Deps.Repo is required") }
    return nil
}
```

Once `Repo` is in `validateDeps`, individual RPC handlers can use
`s.deps.Repo.Get(...)` directly — no `if s.deps.Repo == nil` preamble.
The validation runs once at construction; if it passes, the field is
non-nil for every RPC the service ever handles.

If a Dep really is optional (e.g. an audit sink allowed to be nil in
dev), keep it out of `validateDeps` and check at the one call site that
needs it (mark it `// forge:optional-dep` on the Deps field).

## Component config blocks (configuration ≠ collaborators)

Scalar Deps fields (`MaxPerTick int`, `Interval time.Duration`, ...) are
configuration, not collaborators. Declare a config block in
`proto/<project>/config/v1/config.proto` and take it as one typed field
(`Cfg *config.<Block>Config`, which the graph fills from the loaded config by
type) instead of threading naked scalars through `Deps`. A config block also
narrows what the root that reaches the component needs at startup.
`forge lint --config-deps` flags naked-scalar Deps fields.

## Test wiring

Tests bypass the graph: each test constructs only what it needs. Each
component gets its own generated `helpers_gen_test.go`, in that component's
directory and package — e.g. `internal/handlers/order/helpers_gen_test.go`,
`package order`. It emits `NewTest<Svc>(t, ...opts)` (and, for a handler,
`NewTest<Svc>Server`, which serves it over a real Connect client). Defaults
flow in for the bare-Deps trio, every interface-typed field gets its mock, and
`With<Svc>Deps(deps)` overrides the rich deps.

Two properties of that placement are load-bearing:

- The `_test.go` **suffix** is what keeps package `testing` out of the
  production binary. The toolchain compiles these files only into the
  package's test binary, so `go list -deps ./cmd/<proj>` never contains
  `testing`.
- The **package clause** is the component's own (`package order`), not
  `order_test`. Go compiles a package's in-package `_test.go` files INTO the
  package under test, and an external `order_test` package then imports that
  augmented package — so the helpers are usable from BOTH internal and
  external test files in that directory, and from nowhere else.

Typed entity factories (`New<Entity>(t, db, …)`, a minimal valid row) and
create-request factories (`New<CreateRequest>(t, db, variant)`) land beside the
handler whose CRUD RPCs own that entity, as
`internal/handlers/<svc>/factories_gen_test.go` — regenerated from the applied
schema every run, same package clause, same visibility rule as the harness
above. Tests in that directory call `NewItem(t, db)` unqualified; a test
elsewhere drives the entity through its RPCs rather than seeding rows behind
them.

See the `testing/patterns` skill for the canonical patterns.
